<h2>About Me</h2>
<p>
	My name is Amanda Sebring (she/her) and I am a Licensed Clinical Social Worker
	and clinical supervisor. I primarily offer individual and family therapy to
	adolescents and young adults (approximately ages 14-30). I specialize in the
	treatment of depression, anxiety, and trauma symptoms. Additionally, I have
	extensive experience and am comfortable working with individuals who
	experience suicidal ideation or engage in non-suicidal self-injury
	(self-harm). My goal in therapy is to create a supportive environment in
	which clients feel respected and understood, where they can work toward
	self-identified goals and build a life worth living.
</p>

<h3>Past Experience</h3>
<p>
	I have worked in the mental health field for over 13 years, supporting folks
	across the lifespan.
</p>
<p>
	I spent the early years of my career working with elderly individuals
	experiencing dementia. I truly enjoyed these years, supporting residents with
	activities of daily living, facilitating enriching activities, hearing
	stories about people's lives, and reading residents some beautiful
	poetry that they wrote in their younger years. I noticed that above all,
	family visits were the thing that elicited the most joy in residents.
	Throughout my four years in this role, I supported multiple families through
	the passing of their loved ones. I think back on this time fondly,
	recognizing that this is where my passion for working with families took
	root.
</p>
<p>
	Shortly after earning my bachelor's degree, I returned to my original
	goal and passion, working with youth and their families in a residential
	treatment setting. I worked directly with youth to support skill building,
	crisis management, and safety planning.
</p>
<p>
	After receiving my Master of Social Work degree, I returned to a residential
	treatment setting, this time as a Child and Family Therapist. For multiple
	years, I worked closely with adolescents and their families to improve
	communication in the home, support parents with psychoeducation about their
	child's mental health challenges, engage in collaborative safety
	planning, and worked toward one common goal - to support the youth and
	family with being able to have the tools and resources necessary to safely
	return back to the community.
</p>

